          "worked_solution": "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$P(n):\\,4\\,∣\\,{5}^{2n-1}+3$ for all $n\\in N$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into the expression and show it is divisible by $4:$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$ Substituting $n=1$ into the expression ${5}^{2n-1}+3,$ we have\n\n$$\n{5}^{2(1)-1}+3=8,\\,\\text{and}\\,4\\,∣\\,8.\n$$\n\n \nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(k):\\,4\\,∣\\,{5}^{2k-1}+3.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\nP(k):\\,4\\,∣\\,{5}^{2(k+1)-1}+3.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the expression ${5}^{2(k+1)-1}+3$ as ${5}^{2k-1}+3$ (from the inductive hypothesis) plus a multiple of $4.$ We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2(k+1)-1}+3 & ={5}^{2k-1+2}+3 \\\\  & ={5}^{2k-1}\\cdot {5}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =25({5}^{2k-1})+3 \\\\  & =24({5}^{2k-1})+{5}^{2k-1}+3 \\\\  & =24({5}^{2k-1})+({5}^{2k-1}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $4\\,∣\\,({5}^{2k-1}+3)$ by the inductive hypothesis, and $24({5}^{2k-1})=4\\cdot 6({5}^{2k-1})$ is clearly divisible by $4,$ their sum is also divisible by $4,$ as required.\n\nSince we have shown that the expression in $(∗)$ is divisible by $4,$ our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $4\\,∣\\,{5}^{2n-1}+3$ for all $n\\in N.$",
